  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1009 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nThe overall weather then looks dry for a period through Friday \nnight and Saturday as the upper level flow transitions from \nnorthwesterly (current) to zonal, by Saturday and then \nsouthwesterly for Sunday. The pattern then shifts for Sunday as\nanother area of low pressure swings across the plains and into \nthe Ozarks late Saturday night into Sunday morning. This system\nis expected to remain all rain with temperatures in the 40s \novernight Saturday night and through the day on Sunday. \nPrecipitation amounts have remained fairly consistent with from\n0.10 to 0.25 inches expected with the highest amounts in the\neastern Ozarks. Models are also showing some limited \ninstability for Sunday with from 300 to 700j/kg of CAPE, mainly \nin the eastern Ozarks. This may allow for a few rumbles of \nthunder to occur as well. \n\nSome rain may linger across the area into Sunday afternoon \nbehind the departing area of surface low pressure. Gusty \nnorthwesterly winds will also develop behind the low. Overall the\ntrack and timing of the system has remained consistent with \nrain chances 40 to 80% with limited (10 to 20%) chances for a \nfew embedded thunderstorms. \n\nA warming trend will then occur early next week with highs in\nthe 50s on Monday and Tuesday as highs warm into the 60s for \nthe middle of next week. Highs will be coolest on Sunday with \nreading in the upper 40s to middle 50s.",
